/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"bassstation-level-7","number":7,"title":"Sub-Oscillator, Sync, Paraphonic Mode & Micro-Detuning","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Integrate the dedicated Sub-Oscillator across all waveform selections (Sine, Narrow Pulse, Square) and octave intervals (-1 and -2 octaves relative to Osc 1). - Explain the physical circuitry and acoustic harmonics of Oscillator 1–2 Hard ","modules":[{"id":"bassstation-m-7-1","code":"7","title":"Sub-Oscillator, Sync, Paraphonic Mode & Micro-Detuning","blurb":"- Integrate the dedicated Sub-Oscillator across all waveform selections (Sine, Narrow Pulse, Square) and octave intervals (-1 and -2 octaves relative to Osc 1). - Explain the physical circuitry and acoustic harmonics of Oscillator 1–2 Hard ","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"bassstation-l-o-7","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 7.1 — Extended Oscillator Architecture and Voice Modes","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Section Learning Outcomes","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Integrate the dedicated Sub-Oscillator across all waveform selections (<strong>Sine</strong>, <strong>Narrow Pulse</strong>, <strong>Square</strong>) and octave intervals (<strong>-1</strong> and <strong>-2</strong> octaves relative to Osc 1).","Explain the physical circuitry and acoustic harmonics of <strong>Oscillator 1–2 Hard Synchronization</strong>.","Configure, operate, and evaluate <strong>Paraphonic Mode</strong> (<code>P-1</code>), understanding two-note pitch allocation across a shared filter and VCA.","Program analog pitch drift and vintage instability using the <strong>Oscillator Error</strong> function (<code>E-0</code> to <code>E-7</code>).","Execute independent micro-tonal sub-oscillator detuning via extended Function commands."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-7-1-1","title":"7.1.1: The Sub-Oscillator Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Sub-Oscillator is a dedicated third analog tone generator designed to anchor low-frequency fundamental energy."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                      SUB-OSCILLATOR SIGNAL TOPOLOGY\n   [Oscillator 1 Pitch Core] ──► [Frequency Divider Circuit]\n                                            │\n               ┌────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┐\n               │                                                         │\n       [Sub Octave Switch]                                      [Sub Wave Switch]\n         ├── -1 (Divide frequency by 2: -12 Semitones)            ├── Sine (Pure Fundamental)\n         └── -2 (Divide frequency by 4: -24 Semitones)            ├── Narrow Pulse (Buzzy Sub)\n                                                                 └── Square (Hollow Sub)\n                                            │\n                                            ▼\n                              [Mixer: Sub Level Potentiometer]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Phase-Locked Pitch Tracking</strong>:","The Sub-Oscillator is derived from the master clock / pitch core of <strong>Oscillator 1</strong>.","By default, it locks to the exact frequency and pitch tracking of Oscillator 1 without pitch drift.","<strong>Sub Octave Switch</strong>:","<strong><code>-1</code></strong>: Divides the fundamental frequency of Osc 1 by 2 (drops pitch by exactly 1 octave / 12 semitones).","<strong><code>-2</code></strong>: Divides the fundamental frequency of Osc 1 by 4 (drops pitch by exactly 2 octaves / 24 semitones).","<strong>Sub Waveform Selection</strong>:","<strong>Sine</strong>: A pure, overtone-free sine wave. Adds clean low-end weight without mid-frequency clutter or phase smearing.","<strong>Narrow Pulse</strong>: A narrow rectangular wave rich in buzzy, aggressive harmonics. Cuts through dense mixes on smaller monitoring systems.","<strong>Square</strong>: A symmetrical square wave providing robust odd-harmonic power for traditional dub, acid, and funk basslines."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load Init patch <code>64</code>. In the mixer, turn <strong>Osc 1</strong> to 0 and turn <strong>Sub</strong> to 127.","Switch <strong>Sub Octave</strong> between <code>-1</code> and <code>-2</code> while playing baseline notes; listen to the deep sub-octave drop.","Switch <strong>Sub Wave</strong> between <strong>Sine</strong>, <strong>Narrow Pulse</strong>, and <strong>Square</strong>; observe how the harmonic texture shifts from smooth acoustic bass to buzzy grit."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What oscillator does the Sub-Oscillator track by default?","[ ] What are the three available waveforms for the Sub-Oscillator?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-7-1-2","title":"7.1.2: Oscillator 1–2 Hard Synchronization","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Oscillator Sync (Hard Sync) is an analogue technique that locks the phase cycle of a \"slave\" oscillator (Osc 2) to a \"master\" oscillator (Osc 1)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                 OSCILLATOR HARD SYNC RE-TRIGGERING\n   Osc 1 (Master)  ──/\\────/\\────/\\────/\\────/\\──── (Fixed Pitch Reference)\n                       │     │     │     │     │   (Sync Reset Pulses)\n   Osc 2 (Slave)   ──/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\/\\── (Modulated Pitch / Swept)\n                       │     │     │     │     │\n   Synced Output   ──/\\──/\\/\\/\\──/\\/\\/\\──/\\/\\/\\──── (Complex Timbral Waveform)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Circuit Mechanics</strong>:","Every time the master oscillator (Osc 1) completes a full waveform cycle and crosses zero voltage, it sends a hard reset pulse to the slave oscillator (Osc 2).","The reset pulse forcibly resets Osc 2's waveform back to the beginning of its cycle, regardless of where Osc 2 is in its own period.","<strong>Acoustic Effect &amp; Sound Design Technique</strong>:","If you sweep the pitch of Oscillator 2 (manually, via Mod Env, or via Pitch Wheel) while Sync is engaged, the fundamental pitch heard by the listener <strong>remains locked to Oscillator 1</strong>.","However, the internal forced waveform truncation creates radical, aggressive, ripping, and vocal-like formant shifts.","<strong>Mixer Gain-Staging Rule for Sync</strong>:","When designing classic sync-sweep leads, <strong>turn Oscillator 1 level DOWN or completely OFF in the mixer</strong>, leaving only Oscillator 2 active. This allows the listener to hear the pure, complex synced timbre of Osc 2 without the static fundamental of Osc 1 masking the effect."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On an Init patch, press <strong>Function / Exit</strong> and press the <strong>Osc 1-2 Sync</strong> key (the upper <code>D</code> key).","The display shows <code>On</code> and the physical <strong>Sync LED</strong> in the Oscillator section illuminates. Exit Function mode.","In the Mixer, set <strong>Osc 1 to 0</strong> and <strong>Osc 2 to 127</strong>.","Set Osc 2 to <strong>Sawtooth</strong>.","Play a sustained note and slowly rotate the <strong>Coarse</strong> tuning knob of Osc 2 from -12 to +12.","Hear the ripping, screaming harmonic formant sweeps while the perceived musical note remains locked to your keybed note."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Which oscillator acts as the master and which acts as the slave during Hard Sync?","[ ] Why is Oscillator 1 frequently muted in the mixer when performing hard sync sweeps?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-7-1-3","title":"7.1.3: Paraphonic Mode Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Paraphony allows the Bass Station II to play two independent musical pitches simultaneously while utilizing its single analogue signal path."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                    PARAPHONIC VOICE ALLOCATION MAP\n   Keybed: Two Keys Depressed (e.g., C3 and G3)\n     │\n     ├── Lower Pitch (C3) ──► Routed to [Oscillator 1 Core] ──┐\n     └── Higher Pitch (G3) ──► Routed to [Oscillator 2 Core] ─┼──► [Analog Mixer]\n                                                              │          │\n                                                              │          ▼\n                                                   [Single Shared Filter]\n                                                              │\n                                                              ▼\n                                                   [Single Shared VCA / Amp]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Pitch Routing</strong>:","<strong><code>P-0</code> (Off - Default)</strong>: Standard monophonic mode. Both Osc 1 and Osc 2 track the same keypress.","<strong><code>P-1</code> (On - Paraphonic)</strong>:","Striking a single key assigns that pitch to both oscillators.","Striking two keys assigns the <strong>lower note to Oscillator 1</strong> and the <strong>higher note to Oscillator 2</strong>.","<strong>Shared Filter &amp; VCA Constraints</strong>:","Both oscillator signals are summed into the single mixer, passing through the <strong>one shared analogue filter</strong> and the <strong>one shared analogue VCA</strong>.","If Key 1 is held and Key 2 is struck, Key 2 will articulate through whatever envelope stage is currently active on the shared VCA.","Releasing one key leaves the remaining key sounding without an independent release stage.","<strong>Activation Gesture</strong>:","Press <strong>Function / Exit</strong>, then <strong>double-tap the Osc 1-2 Sync key</strong> (upper <code>D</code>).","The display shows <code>P-0</code> or <code>P-1</code>. Use <strong>Patch/Value ▲</strong> to toggle to <code>P-1</code>.","Paraphonic mode is saved per-patch into memory."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load Init patch <code>64</code>. Ensure both Osc 1 and Osc 2 are set to Sawtooth, 8' Range, with equal mixer levels.","Enable Paraphonic Mode (<code>P-1</code>) using the double-tap gesture.","Play a two-note chord (e.g., <code>C3</code> and <code>E3</code>). Verify that a true musical major third chord sounds.","Release the top note (<code>E3</code>) while holding <code>C3</code>; hear the sound collapse back to unison single-note playback."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What is the exact multi-press gesture required to enter the Paraphonic mode submenu?","[ ] How are notes allocated between Osc 1 and Osc 2 when two keys are struck in paraphonic mode?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-7-1-4","title":"7.1.4: Oscillator Error (Analogue Drift Simulation)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Modern DCOs (Digitally Controlled Oscillators) are exceptionally stable. To recreate the subtle pitch instability, temperature drift, and component tolerance variations of vintage 1970s discrete VCOs, the Bass Station II provides the <strong>Oscillator Error</strong> function."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    OSCILLATOR ERROR PARAMETER VALUES                       │\n├───────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Setting       │ Operational Behavior &amp; Drift Range                         │\n├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ `E-0`         │ Zero drift (Perfect digital pitch locking). Default.       │\n├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ `E-1` to `E-3`│ Subtle organic pitch drift (Authentic vintage warmth).     │\n├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ `E-4` to `E-6`│ Moderate detuning &amp; random phase slurring.                 │\n├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ `E-7`         │ Maximum random pitch error (Up to ~1 semitone drift).      │\n└───────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Drift Algorithm</strong>:","Introduces controlled, non-linear, pseudorandom micro-tonal pitch offsets on every note-on event.","In Paraphonic mode (<code>P-1</code>), Oscillator Error applies <strong>independent, unsynchronized random drift values</strong> to Osc 1 and Osc 2, creating authentic dual-voice analog variance.","<strong>Activation Gesture</strong>:","Press <strong>Function / Exit</strong>, then <strong>press the Pitch Bend Range key twice</strong> (the lower <code>D</code> key).","Display shows <code>E-0</code> to <code>E-7</code>. Adjust using <strong>Patch/Value ▲ and ▼</strong>.","Setting is saved per-patch."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On an initialized patch with both Osc 1 and Osc 2 active, access the Oscillator Error menu.","Set the value to <code>E-3</code>. Play rapid staccato notes; listen to the organic, lively analog phase interaction on each note strike.","Set the value to <code>E-7</code>; play notes and hear the extreme detuning variance. Reset back to <code>E-0</code>."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-7-1-5","title":"7.1.5: Extended Sub-Oscillator Tuning and Detune","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.5: Extended Sub-Oscillator Tuning and Detune","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"1. Technical Exposition","level":4},{"type":"prose","html":"While the Sub-Oscillator is locked to Oscillator 1's fundamental pitch by default, the v5.0 firmware architecture allows the Sub-Oscillator to be <strong>independently detuned</strong> relative to Osc 1."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                      EXTENDED SUB-OSCILLATOR TUNING\n   [Function Held] + Rotate [Coarse Pot] ──► Sub-Osc Coarse Detune (Semitones)\n   [Function Held] + Rotate [Fine Pot]   ──► Sub-Osc Fine Detune (Cents)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Operational Procedure</strong>:","Press and hold <strong>Function / Exit</strong>.","While holding Function, rotate the <strong>Coarse</strong> or <strong>Fine</strong> tuning potentiometer in the Oscillator section.","The display indicates the sub-oscillator detuning offset.","A value of <code>0</code> restores default phase-locked tracking with Osc 1.","<strong>Sound Design Utility</strong>:","Allows the Sub-Oscillator to be tuned to a Fifth (<code>+7</code> semitones) or micro-detuned by a few cents against both primary oscillators, creating massive 3-oscillator wall-of-sound lead and bass textures."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","level":4},{"type":"list","items":["In the mixer, bring up <strong>Osc 1</strong> (100) and <strong>Sub</strong> (100).","Hold <strong>Function</strong> and rotate the <strong>Fine</strong> knob slightly clockwise (offsetting Sub by <code>+6</code> cents).","Release Function. Play a note; hear the Sub-Oscillator chorus against Oscillator 1 independently of Oscillator 2."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Assessment Gate D — Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before advancing to Module 8, verify complete mastery of Gate D proficiencies:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] <strong>Two-Oscillator + Sub Sound</strong>: Build a patch utilizing Osc 1 (Saw, 8'), Osc 2 (Saw, 8', detuned +8 cents), and Sub-Oscillator (Square, -1 octave).","[ ] <strong>Hard Sync Demonstration</strong>: Enable Osc 1–2 Sync, mute Osc 1 in the mixer, and execute a manual sync formant sweep using Osc 2 Coarse tuning.","[ ] <strong>Paraphonic Mode Execution</strong>: Access the <code>P-0/P-1</code> menu using the double-tap gesture, enable <code>P-1</code>, and demonstrate two-note chord playback on the physical keybed.","[ ] <strong>Oscillator Error Configuration</strong>: Access the <code>E-0…E-7</code> menu and explain its impact on both monophonic and paraphonic voice allocation.","[ ] <strong>Sub-Osc Detuning</strong>: Apply micro-tonal detuning to the Sub-Oscillator using the Function + Fine combination and restore it to <code>0</code>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
